<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Kursk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> was fought on the Eastern Front in July and August 1943, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> attacked the Soviet salient around Kursk in Operation Citadel. Prepared Soviet defences halted the offensive within days, and Soviet counter-offensives retook Oryol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a>. One of the largest battles in history, with great tank clashes such as Prokhorovka, it was the last major German offensive in the east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kursk.jpg" alt="Battle of Kursk" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Kursk_(map).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> moved to a general offensive in the south, and by February 1943 the southern sector of the German front was in strategic crisis. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> was retaken by the Soviets on 8 February. Short of infantry and artillery, the Germans would have to rely chiefly on their panzer divisions for any offensive in 1943. On 10 March Manstein presented a plan to pinch off the Kursk salient. Hitler believed that a victory would reassert German strength and improve his prestige with allies he thought were considering withdrawing from the war.</p><p>The Soviets had foreknowledge of the German plans through the Lucy spy ring in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and the spy John Cairncross at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bletchley-park">Bletchley Park</a>. Georgiy Zhukov and other officers recommended a strategic defensive before going on the offensive. The Germans delayed the attack to build up their forces, which gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> time to construct a series of deep defensive belts and a large reserve.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Operation Citadel began on 5 July 1943, with attacks from north and south on the base of the salient. In the north, Model's 9th Army attacked the Central Front, and the fighting around Ponyri and Olkhovatka became a battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> that troops called a mini-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a>. In the south, Hoth's 4th Panzer Army advanced against the Voronezh Front, and the Soviets committed their strategic reserves, including the 5th Guards Tank Army, which advanced to Prokhorovka, where a large armoured clash took place from 12 July.</p><p>The Western Allies invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> on 10 July, and on 12 July Hitler decided to stop the offensive and move forces to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, though Manstein objected that his forces were on the verge of breaking through. The same day the Soviets launched Operation Kutuzov against the Orel salient, and on 3 August they began Operation Polkovodets Rumyantsev, liberating Belgorod by 5 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>For the first time a major German offensive was stopped before it broke through; the Germans advanced at most 8 to 12 kilometres in the north and 35 kilometres in the south. The Soviet victory was costly, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>'s industry and manpower allowed it to replace its losses. The battle was the last strategic offensive the Germans were able to launch on the Eastern Front, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> held the strategic initiative for the rest of the war. It is the single largest battle in the history of warfare and the single deadliest armoured battle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Kursk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Defeat at the Battle of Stalingrad, where the Sixth Army surrendered in early 1943, and the failed offensive at the <b>Battle of Kursk</b> that July lost the initiative in the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Second World War it bore much of the fighting after Germany invaded on 22 June 1941: Leningrad endured a siege of almost 900 days, the Germans were stopped before Moscow in December 1941, and the battles of Stalingrad in 1942–1943 and <b>Kursk</b> in 1943 turned the war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…catastrophic losses, but the Red Army halted the Germans at Moscow in December 1941, destroyed an entire German army at Stalingrad in 1942–1943, won the tank battle at <b>Kursk</b> in July 1943 and smashed Army Group Centre in Operation Bagration in 1944, before taking Berlin in May 1945.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Military</a></li></ul></section>
